      <p>The breakdown of first addresses: 7 used an AWS hostname, 6 used an Alibaba Cloud hostname, 20 were local or internal-looking, and 11 of the public-looking domains did not exist. Check Point also tested a smaller change: swapping the order of <code>curl</code> and <code>wget</code> in the tool-availability check reduced the number of completions containing a curl network command from 15 out of 32 to 4 out of 32, despite both lists containing the same programs. The model's behavior pivoted on which tool name appeared first.</p>
      <p>Check Point's conclusion: "model jaggedness" — inconsistent behavior that changes with small differences in context — was "the most likely explanation." They added that Flash-Next is an experimental preview of the Qwen4 architecture and that "rough edges are plausible in an experimental checkpoint."</p>
      <p>But Check Point's own text states the critical limit of their experiment. Their replay starts after the model has already committed to a curl command. They tested where the model points, not how often the model fires in the first place. "Both tests probe what are the possible scenarios that can happen once the model has decided to produce a tool call," Check Point wrote. "They cannot tell us how often the model would initiate an unrequested upload or why it chose to use a tool call in the first place." And: "While these results do not prove that there is no backdoor (which is extremely difficult to do), it hints at the most likely explanation simply being model jaggedness." The rebuttal narrows the question. It does not close it.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="inside-the-weights">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — The Mechanism</span>Inside the Weights</h2>
      <p>Two deeper investigations tried to explain not just where the model points its requests, but why the behavior exists at all.</p>
      <p>Cohere's prefill experiment walked the model's own trace forward one curl flag at a time, sampling hundreds of completions at each step. The results, which have not been independently replicated, showed a sharp threshold. Before the <code>-o</code> flag — which tells curl to save a file to disk — only 4% of completions generated a URL at all, and 0.5% hit a watchlist host. After <code>-o</code>, URL generation jumped to 92% and watchlist hits climbed to 23%, led by the routify host. Separately, the cloud metadata address 169.254.169.254 — where AWS, Google Cloud, and Azure machines store temporary IAM credentials — appeared in 23% to 36% of completions before the <code>-o</code> trigger. Cohere read this as an attempted credential grab, with two caveats of its own: "our isolated sandbox had nothing live to return, and a reflex this rare doesn't prove intent."</p>
